WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.030
Part 3 of nine: Make language assistance usable.

2
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.580
The standards connected with this part are 5, 6, 7.

3
00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:11.390
Here is the work this part helps you prepare: Trace

4
00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:13.950
an assistance request from offer through follow-up

5
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.660
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 5 is described as:

6
00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.900
Timely language assistance without cost to the individual.

7
00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:27.370
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 6 is described as:

8
00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:30.310
Oral and written information about available language assistance.

9
00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:35.680
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 7 is described as:

10
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.560
Competent language assistance.

11
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.100
Follow the request through.

12
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.840
What makes language assistance usable, beyond having it listed as

13
00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:45.580
an available service?

14
00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:50.570
Language assistance is useful when someone can find it, request

15
00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.910
it and receive it in time to participate. In this

16
00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.970
lesson, we will connect standards five, six and seven to

17
00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.030
the steps before, during and after an encounter.

18
00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.900
Standard five addresses timely language assistance without cost to the

19
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.660
individual. Standard six addresses making people aware of that assistance

20
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:11.140
orally and in writing. Standard seven addresses the competence of

21
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.000
people providing language assistance. The standards work together: an available

22
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.400
service does little good if someone does not know about

23
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.760
it or cannot request it.

24
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:23.290
For your specific work, use current agency procedures and the

25
00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:27.330
responsible program's guidance. This lesson does not establish a new

26
00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:32.910
vendor, reimbursement rule, eligibility rule or approval process. Knowing how

27
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.090
to locate the right procedure is part of being prepared.

28
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.560
Before the encounter.

29
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:42.110
What should we check before the service-planning conversation begins?

30
00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:46.220
Return to the service-planning notice. It gives a date and

31
00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.680
a number to call, but does not explain how to

32
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.180
request assistance. A practical review asks whether the notice makes

33
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.040
assistance visible, whether the request method is usable, and whether

34
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.660
someone is responsible for responding.

35
00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:03.370
Ask about preferred language and communication needs respectfully, using an

36
00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:06.970
approved process. Do not assume that a person wants an

37
00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:10.170
interpreter because of their name or accent, or assume that

38
00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.990
they do not because they can hold a brief conversation

39
00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:17.330
in English. Understanding everyday conversation and understanding a complex service

40
00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:18.830
decision are different tasks.

41
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.000
When an interpreter is needed, follow the applicable arrangements early

42
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.040
enough to support participation. Confirm the language, any relevant communication

43
00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.660
needs, the setting, timing and the information required for the

44
00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:34.220
assignment. Share only information that is appropriate and necessary through

45
00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.160
the approved channels.

46
00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.880
A request is not complete merely because it was sent;

47
00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.460
someone needs to confirm the arrangement and know what to

48
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:42.060
do if it fails.

49
00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:45.610
Support people and interpreter roles.

50
00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.160
How do we respect a chosen support person without confusing

51
00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:50.640
their role with an interpreter's role?

52
00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:54.150
Consider a person who arrives with a family member and

53
00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.370
says that the family member has been helping with communication.

54
00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.210
Treat both people respectfully. Do not assume that the family

55
00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.470
member is qualified to interpret complex information, and do not

56
00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.770
confuse the person's chosen support with the organization's responsibility to

57
00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.490
provide appropriate communication assistance.

58
00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:13.700
The CLAS framework discourages using untrained individuals and minors as

59
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:19.320
interpreters. Applicable laws and agency procedures also matter. Follow those

60
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.340
requirements and seek the appropriate guidance rather than making a

61
00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.800
blanket decision based only on this example. Explain the available

62
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.000
assistance, ask about the person's preferences, and preserve their involvement

63
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:30.640
in decisions.

64
00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:34.930
A qualified interpreter and a chosen support person can have

65
00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:39.170
different roles. One conveys communication accurately; the other may provide

66
00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:42.950
the support the person wants. Clarifying roles helps avoid putting

67
00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:45.470
someone in a position they are not prepared or authorized

68
00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:45.830
to fill.

69
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.640
The current HHS standard also connects competence with training and,

70
00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.800
where available, certification. These belong in the appropriate arrangements for

71
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.380
assistance; the learning activity does not itself qualify anyone to

72
00:03:58.380 --> 00:03:58.700
interpret.

73
00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:01.230
During the encounter.

74
00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.780
Once the conversation starts, what should the staff member keep

75
00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:06.060
checking?

76
00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:11.330
Address the person directly. Use clear language and manageable portions

77
00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:15.530
of information. Allow time for interpretation and questions. Avoid speaking

78
00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:18.330
about the person as though they are not present. Check

79
00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:21.650
whether the communication method is working, and respond if there

80
00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:22.130
is a problem.

81
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.180
Checking understanding should not feel like testing or blaming the

82
00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.820
person. You can explain that you want to check whether

83
00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:31.520
your explanation of the next step was clear, then invite

84
00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.140
them to explain what they understand will happen. If the

85
00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.400
explanation was unclear, try again in a different way. Do

86
00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.540
not simply repeat the same wording more loudly.

87
00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:44.690
If assistance is unavailable or unsuitable, follow the approved contingency

88
00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:48.210
and escalation process. Do not improvise with a child, an

89
00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:51.990
unapproved automated tool, or an unqualified bystander to complete a

90
00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:55.070
consequential conversation. Explain what you can do now and how

91
00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:58.610
the communication need will be addressed. Urgent circumstances require the

92
00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:02.050
applicable urgent-response procedure, not a rule invented by this lesson.

93
00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:04.280
After the encounter.

94
00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:08.390
And what does follow-through look like after that conversation?

95
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.260
Confirm the next step in a form the person can

96
00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:17.280
use. Record operational information required by the applicable procedure, using

97
00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.420
approved systems and appropriate privacy protections. A learning worksheet is

98
00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:22.240
not a case record.

99
00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:26.510
If there was a delay or repeated difficulty arranging assistance,

100
00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:30.950
raise that pattern through the appropriate channel. Individual staff should

101
00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:33.870
not have to solve the same preventable problem repeatedly without

102
00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:37.810
organizational support. That connection will matter when we discuss leadership,

103
00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:39.230
resources and improvement.

104
00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.780
Practice and carry it forward.

105
00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:45.870
Let's trace the whole assistance pathway in our own example.

106
00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:51.020
In the companion activity, decide which next step best supports

107
00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.660
a person whose assistance request has not been confirmed. Look

108
00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.300
for an answer that checks the arrangement, communicates with the

109
00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.580
person and uses the appropriate escalation route. An answer that

110
00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.860
simply assumes someone else handled it leaves the barrier unresolved.

111
00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:08.670
For your work example, identify where assistance is offered, how

112
00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:12.030
it is requested, who responds and how completion is confirmed.

113
00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:14.450
If you do not know one of those steps, record

114
00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:17.690
a question for the responsible area. Do not enter anyone's

115
00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:18.390
personal details.

116
00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:21.440
You now have a way to examine the whole request,

117
00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.660
rather than just the presence of an assistance statement. Next,

118
00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:27.520
we will look at the information itself: whether someone can

119
00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:30.540
find the message, access it, understand it, use it and

120
00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:31.100
act on it.

121
00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:36.250
Let's work through the companion example: A request needs follow-through

122
00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.140
Here is the situation. An assistance request has been forwarded,

123
00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:43.020
but the arrangement has not been confirmed before the planned

124
00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:43.500
conversation.

125
00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.210
What response would move this example forward responsibly?

126
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.340
The responsible staff member checks the request through the approved

127
00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:56.600
route, confirms the arrangement or escalates the gap, and communicates

128
00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.860
the next step to the person in a usable way.

129
00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:03.830
And here is why that response fits. Availability, awareness and

130
00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:07.490
competent assistance need to work together. Sending a request does

131
00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:09.390
not establish that assistance will be provided.

132
00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:14.160
Now make your own choice. An assistance request was sent,

133
00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:16.820
but no one has confirmed the arrangement for a planned

134
00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:18.900
conversation. What should happen next?

135
00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:24.130
Option 1. Check the arrangement through the approved process, communicate

136
00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:27.230
with the person and use the appropriate escalation route if

137
00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:27.450
needed.

138
00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:31.140
Option 2. Assume the request was handled because it was

139
00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:32.180
sent to the usual address.

140
00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:36.790
Option 3. Plan to ask any available family member to

141
00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:38.370
interpret if assistance is unavailable.

142
00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:41.540
Pause the player now if you want to choose and

143
00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:44.800
explain your reasoning. You can use your own notes or

144
00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.680
the companion activity. When you're ready, resume and we'll work

145
00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:49.820
through all three responses.

146
00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:52.790
How should we think about option 1?

147
00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.860
A sent request is not a confirmed arrangement. Follow the

148
00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:00.600
applicable procedure and make sure the person knows what will

149
00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:04.180
happen. Do not promise a service or change outside your

150
00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:04.500
authority.

151
00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:08.590
Option 1 is the supported choice in this activity.

152
00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:11.420
How should we think about option 2?

153
00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:17.110
That leaves the communication need unconfirmed. Someone needs responsibility for

154
00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:19.670
follow-through and a contingency if the arrangement fails.

155
00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:22.360
How should we think about option 3?

156
00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:26.890
A support person's role is not automatically an interpreter's role.

157
00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:32.010
Follow the applicable requirements for appropriate assistance rather than improvising

158
00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.430
with an unqualified person.

159
00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:37.020
You can choose another response and compare the reasoning.

160
00:08:37.790 --> 00:08:41.090
For your example, where is assistance offered, how is it

161
00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:45.430
requested, who responds and how is completion confirmed? Note gaps

162
00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:47.050
as questions, not guessed procedures.

163
00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:51.640
The working note from this part is: Assistance pathway

164
00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:54.830
Pause the player if you want time to make that

165
00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:58.510
note. Keep it general, without names or private details. Thinking

166
00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:00.350
it through without writing is also fine.

167
00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:04.220
That completes part 3. You can return to any passage

168
00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:06.900
or take a break. The next part is Make information

169
00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:08.220
accessible and actionable.
